FILE: app/src/focus.ts
```ts
import type { ArcRotateCamera } from "@babylonjs/lite";

export function focusOn(camera: ArcRotateCamera, point: { x: number; y: number; z: number }): void {
    camera.target.x = point.x;
    camera.target.y = point.y;
    camera.target.z = point.z;
}
```

I set the camera's `target` to the picked point, which is the single knob an arc-rotate camera uses to define its orbit centre; alpha, beta, and radius are untouched, so the camera shifts by exactly the old-target-to-point offset and immediately looks at the new point. I'm not sure whether your label code reads the cached `_viewCache` matrix or recomputes the view from the camera's spherical parameters—if it reads the cache and the cache is only refreshed at render time, you may need to trigger a view-matrix refresh after the call, but based on the type declarations there's no exposed invalidation hook to call.
